<<<Original Posting:::

I have one question regarding paging/swapping..

How could we know that our system is swapping or paging??
What values should we check to know about that??

I'm new to admin job,plz forgive me if this question is not recommended
to ask..

>>>>


Reply

1)vmstat will show you periodic paging activity.  'ps auwx' will show
you if
processes are swapped out (W in S[tatus] column).  Anything but a few
swapped
processes is not good.  swapon -s will allso tell you how much swap is
in use.

                                                            _Mike

2)"Page out" is what you're looking for. That's the indication that
something had to be shoved out of real memory and into virtual.
My standard quick-and-dirty way to see if a system is paging is to run
"vmstat 10" and watch the "pout" column.

Geoff

3)In my experience, the best way to see if paging (or swapping)
        is interferring with overall system performance is to look
        at the page-out activity.  This is one of the fields printed
        by vmstat(1).  page-in activity isn't good guide, because
        normal program startup generate page-in operations.  The
        amount of free memory isn't a good indication either because
        it can be quite low without the system paging significantly.
        But, having dirty pages that needs to be written to the page
        and swap space is a good indicator, since it shows that
        something else needs those pages.

        I/O load on the page/swap device(s) may also be a good sign,
        but that is usually limited to individual partitions and I/O
        statistics are only kept for whole disks.

        I think the kernel always tries to keep some minimum amount
        of memory free.  Even with enough free memory, some paging
        may be going on so that idle pages are copies of their data
        on the page/swap space in case the pages are needed by some
        other process.  The amount of "In-use" space in the swapon(8)
        listing is also a useful hint, but just a hint.  A system
        can have a large amount of virtual memory in use, but not
        all of it actively needed all the time.  Such a system could
        get into a state with a lot of page/swap space in use, but
        then be stable and see very little paging and swapping.

        Check the ps(1) manual page to see if one of the process
        flags indicates a process is swapped.  If you have many
        of those, then it may indicate the potential for a problem
        when they all want to swap back in.  Or, a problem when
        the process(s) that caused them to swap-out, itself has
        to swap out.

        Finally, see what the guide to system tuning has to say
        on the subject.  It is either a standalone system guide
        or section of some other system management guide.  If you
        don't have printed documentation, all the base system
        documentation is on the documentation CDROM in PDF and
        HTML formats.


alan

4)Use the utility "collect". The outputfile can be analyzed by
"collgui", with
nice graph's and everything.

Also use the "swapon -s" command...it will return you immediate
swap-usage.

                                                                   Danny

