Thanks to all that replied, Dr. Tom Blinn's answer helped with booting =
the system without the sysconfigtab file being loaded. (See Below).=A0 =
The answer was to boot from CD, open a terminal, run MAKEDEV to create =
the device files, run fsck against the root disk, mount the root disk, =
cd /etc, copy a previously saved version of the sysconfigtab file into =
place and reboot the machine.=A0 The answer did help me diagnose another =
issue as well.
=A0
=A0
Thanks,
Charles
=A0
=A0
1)=A0 It's MUCH easier to capture the real error messages if you can set =
up to run with a serial console and log things from, say, a PC or =
another UNIX system (e.g., via a "tip" session).
=A0
2)=A0 You have probably changed the /etc/sysconfigtab file.=A0 There is =
a way to tell the kernel to ignore that file with boot flags.=A0 But I =
can't find the documentation -- I *think* it's the "n" flag.=A0 No, from =
src/kernel/arch/alpha/alpha_init.c I find this:

=A0
=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0 =
case 'C':
=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0 =
case 'c':
=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=
=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0 /*
=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=
=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0 * ignore the configuration defaults
=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=
=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0 * stored in /etc/sysconfigtab
=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0 =
=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0*/
=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=
=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0 sysconfigtab =3D NULL;
=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=
=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0 break;
=A0
So try "boot -flags sc" (single user mode, ignore the sysconfigtab) and =
you can put your sysconfigtab changes back to the originals (you did =
save a backup copy of the file, right?).
