Thanks to everyone for the quick responses; Dr Thomas Blinn, Jerry =
Butler,
George Gallen, Kai Grunau and Oisin McGuinness.

I did have an error in my original question as caught by the =
responsers.
Added below are their answers and my question.

> Hi everyone,
>=20
> How do you tar to a remote tape device?
>=20
> I have a directory /data5/p/tmp on a local box, catdog. This =
directory
> contains about 24 gigs of files (8 files from 1-4 gigs in size). I =
want to
> tar this to another server's tape drive; doggie:/dev/tape/tape0_d1
>=20
> I tried the following, but it is way too slow:
>=20
> catdog>   rsh doggie dd of=3D/dev/tape/tape0_d1 | tar cvf - =
/data5/p/tmp
>=20
> Any suggestions or advice is appreciated.

You have the pipe backwards:

	tar cvf - /data5/p/tmp | rsh doggie dd of=3D/dev/tape/tape0_d1

To make it faster, you need to block the tar output and block the=20
writes to the tape:

	tar -b32 -cvf /data5/p/tmp | rsh doggie dd of=3D/dev/tape/tape0_d1
bs=3D64k=20

for instance, although you also need to experiment with reading it
back from the remote system.

You will do reasonably well even if you don't block the tar output,=20
but do block the tape writes.  The tape writes will usually be the
bottleneck in such an arrangement.

