George=20

=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D
from Kai Grunau


tar -cvf - . | rsh doggie dd of=3D/devtape/tape0_d1 bs=3D10240

=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D
from Oisin McGuinness

What you have written:

catdog>   rsh doggie dd of=3D/dev/tape/tape0_d1 | tar cvf - =
/data5/p/tmp

doesn't do anything; the dd on the remote host will wait forever
for input (of specifies output file, so input defaults to stdin), and =
the
tar cvf - creates output but it doesn't go to input anywhere.

Did you intend:

catdog> tar cvf - /data5/p/tmp | rsh doggie dd of=3D/dev/tape/tape0_d1

?
This should have written data, but the blocking might not be optimal;
tar's blocking factor is 20, whereas=A0dd's block size is 512bytes; you
can improve by arranging for the blocking of dd to match that of tar =
output
by setting bs=3D10240. That should help.

You could also consider doing a compression on the creation side, =
followed
by a reblocking before feeding to rsh. (However if your network is fast
and your drive does hardware compression, it might not help.)

catdog> tar cvf - /data5/p/tmp | compress -c - | dd bs=3D10240 | rsh =
doggie
"dd bs=3D10240 of=3D/dev/tape/tape0_d1"

Hope this helps...


